@kenkendk we're currently relying on the `DirectorySeparator` inside `systemInfo` response

```ts
pathDelimiter = computed(() => this.#sysInfo.systemInfo()?.DirectorySeparator ?? '');
```

```ts
this.#isFolder(rootPath, pathDelimiter) ? 'folder' : 'file'
```

```ts
#isFolder(path: string, pathDelimiter: string): boolean {
  return (path.startsWith('%') && path.endsWith('%')) || path.endsWith(pathDelimiter);
}
```

Do you have a good idea if its still a client level issue ?

I followed up in Chromium on ngclient main [`20d6c2128e6c5166ced84784385064b7ff788157`](https://github.com/duplicati/ngclient/commit/20d6c2128e6c5166ced84784385064b7ff788157) against an isolated Windows server running Duplicati `2.4.1.101_canary_2026-11-07`.

In the manual destination editor, I typed `C` → `C:` → `C:\` → `C:\Backup`. The field matched the input at every step, with no extra leading slash. The save returned HTTP 200, the server stored `file:///C%3A%5CBackup`, and the editor still showed `C:\Backup` after a page reload.

@ts678, could you retest on a current build? If the extra slash no longer appears in your setup and the path is preserved after saving, I suggest closing this issue.

