We currently have options/destination options etc but they dont do any validation besides the enumerations naterual validation by only showing allowed options

I think we should agree on a format/contract between the frontend and backend so when we consume the options on the frontend it also carries a validation object like the example below are the current + the validation suggestion

```typescript
// This is the generated types from the backend
export type ArgumentType = 'String' | 'Integer' | 'Boolean' | 'Timespan' | 'Size' | 'Enumeration' | 'Path' | 'Password' | 'Flags' | 'Decimal' | 'Unknown';


export type ICommandLineArgument = {
    Aliases?: Array<(string)> | null;
    LongDescription?: (string) | null;
    Name?: (string) | null;
    ShortDescription?: (string) | null;
    Type?: ArgumentType;
    ValidValues?: Array<(string)> | null;
    DefaultValue?: (string) | null;
    readonly Typename?: (string) | null;
    Deprecated?: boolean;
    DeprecationMessage?: (string) | null;

    // Suggestion
    Validations: [
      { 
         Type: ValidationType; // Min, Max, Pattern, Custom
         Value: string | number | ...; // Should be the type that corresponds to the validation type
      }
    ]
};
``` 

This is so that when we need to update validation on a provider or add a new provider we know validation are aligned across frontend and backend

But where we ofc dont ship validation that we dont need like for Enumerations and Flags, in general i think we should only ship validation if we found a case where it really makes sense like for #118
